vailable how large does China as a  potential market for America loomed for  the future well I don't know anybody who  knows I think there were a lot of a lot  of estimates made early on even last  December in January many people will be  of course visiting the People's Republic  of China many business leaders farmers  everybody looking for piece of the  action because there are billion people  there but I think dung shopping himself  has at least tried to balance that by  indicating that and they've recently  can't cancel the contracts with Japan  which is an indication that they're  certainly willing and in the bill passed  in the Congress in 78 called the export  stimulation act we provided for the  first time the authorization for credits  the People's Republic of China for  certain agriculture commodity so and  they're also in the in the process of  maybe some long term agreements as far  as the sale again of agricultural  products so everything there are a lot  of things working but I would be the  first to say that is probably not going  to be quite as grandiose as some they  may have anticipated they have the  people there may be a demand but I'm not  certain they have the resources now if  we can work out some exchange something  they have that might help to my  astonishment I find myself in full  agreement with Bob Dole let me get back  to what bob was saying many people who  live in the eastern part of the United  States have no idea of the tremendous  depth that we Westerners owe the Chinese  in fact I doubt that California or  Arizona would be as far along without  the help we received from those people  and I get back to the fact that we're  talking about Chinese  and they're all basically decent lovable  people the only group that we've had  arguments with have been those who took  over the government and made a communist  government out of it  but I think from the standpoint of  culture mutual education that we can  gain a lot probably before we ever