f  men can make decisions for whatever  reasons it may not be in the best  interest of the people that they govern  and it certainly may not be in the best  interest of the people of our country I  think this is true of practically any  country in the world we say we don't  like the Russians we don't we wouldn't  trust the Russians I'd trust an  individual Russian I wouldn't trust  anybody in the port borough and I  wouldn't trust anybody in Peking in the  government but I trust the people that  live out in Kunming and chungqing and  other places in China what we say we  don't like people I know Bob is not  referring to the broad great spectrum of  Chinese people if you've ever known them  and lived with them as I have you love  my hell of a long time before you get  mad at me all right next question please  yes sir Jeff Gaynor director of foreign  policy story at the Heritage Foundation  my questions for congressman Bingham in  his statement earlier he alluded to the  statements that Dunc Xiao ping is made  about allowing Taiwan to continue to  have their own economic system possibly  your own military forces I just wonder  if he's aware of any time that this has  ever been said by dong in which he did  not have offered the precondition that  they must abandon essentially their  their claim as a government take down  their flag break diplomatic relations  with all their governments and effects  say that Pei King is the sovereign over  Taiwan and if these are the conditions  for it just how meaningful is this  especially since later on you yourself  said you did not believe you in the  future what would happen in the PRC in  regards to leadership changes how Taiwan  could rely upon something like this  simply because of certain statements  dong is made well as I think I said in  my statement earlier Dunn made that  statement in relation to the question if  Taiwan and Peking get together and can  resolve their differences under what  conditions would you contemplate 